ROLE
You are writing a scholarly article on [TOPIC] for a specialist
readership in phenomenology. The attached notes are your working
materials. Produce a finished article — not an outline, not a
summary of the notes.

SOURCE DISCIPLINE (overrides everything below)
- The notes are a closed corpus. Every textual claim must be
  traceable to a passage actually cited in them.
- Never supply a citation from memory. If you know a relevant
  passage exists but it is not in the notes, do not cite it —
  log it in the Research Register instead.
- Quote German/French in the original where the notes give it,
  with translation.
- Keep three tiers distinct: (a) what the text says, (b) what the
  notes' commentary claims, (c) what you are arguing. (c) must
  never borrow the authority of (a).
- If an argumentative step needs a text you don't have, write the
  step and mark it [UNSUPPORTED — needs X]. Do not paper over it.

ARGUMENT
- The article must advance a thesis the notes do not already
  state — something they make possible but do not make. If the
  strongest available thesis is that the notes' own synthesis is
  underdetermined or wrong, argue that.
- Reconstruct the strongest opposing reading before rejecting it.
- Date doctrinal shifts across a corpus; do not resolve a tension
  the texts leave open.

FORM
- Continuous prose. Section breaks only where the argument turns.
- No "in this article I will", no closing recapitulation, no
  bullet lists.
- Citation convention: [YOUR CONVENTION]
- Length: [N] words.

OUTPUT FILE
- Save the finished article to articles/<slug>-article.md — the
  top-level articles/ folder, not under commentaries/. Articles are
  a separate category from the per-author commentaries.
- Slug: <topic>-article, using the same slugging rules as
  prompts/filename-prompt.md (lowercase, hyphens, transliterated
  diacritics, punctuation dropped rather than hyphenated, subtitle
  dropped after a colon or dash) — e.g.
  heidegger-husserl-intentionality-article.md.
- Frontmatter, before the title heading:
  ---
  layout: default
  title: <human-readable title>
  ---
  Quote the title in YAML (single quotes, doubling any internal
  apostrophe) if it contains a colon — e.g. title: 'Intentionality
  Objectivated: Heidegger''s Terminological Inheritance, 1921–1927'.

RESEARCH REGISTER (append after the article)
1. Works and passages actually used, grouped by text.
2. Desiderata: what's missing, and what each would settle.
3. Load-bearing points resting on the notes' commentary with no
   primary text behind them.
4. Directions opened but not taken.
5. Places the notes appear wrong or internally in tension.
